intr_rule, on the extracted code.

Charon cannot take an inherent method as an entry point, so the crate root
carries a free function per method and those are the names the manifest counts.
A wrapper that quietly did something other than call through would make every
theorem about the method a statement about code nobody runs, so each one is
proven to be its method below.

These are generated by tools/extraction/mirror_crate.py and are the floor, not
the point. The properties that matter go beneath them, and the shapes most of
them take are stated once in NonosExtraction.Shapes.
-/

import NonosExtraction.AmdViIntrRule

open Aeneas Aeneas.Std Result
open nonos_x_amd_vi_intr_rule

set_option linter.hashCommand false
set_option maxRecDepth 100000

namespace NonosExtraction.AmdViIntrRule

/-! ### The forwarding functions add nothing -/

theorem the_remapped_wrapper_is_its_method (a : Std.U64) :
    remapped a = intr_rule.remapped a := rfl

theorem the_passes_any_wrapper_is_its_method (a : Std.U64) :
    passes_any a = intr_rule.passes_any a := rfl

theorem the_irte_wrapper_is_its_method (a : Std.U8) (b : Std.U8) :
    irte a b = intr_rule.irte a b := rfl

/-! ### Axiom profile -/

#print axioms NonosExtraction.AmdViIntrRule.the_remapped_wrapper_is_its_method
#print axioms NonosExtraction.AmdViIntrRule.the_passes_any_wrapper_is_its_method
#print axioms NonosExtraction.AmdViIntrRule.the_irte_wrapper_is_its_method

end NonosExtraction.AmdViIntrRule
